Title:
Windows 11 LPE Sweep: SYSTEM Scheduled Task Writable by Users (MareBackup), World-Writable Named Pipes w/ SID Spoofing, and Posture of Common Local Privesc Surfaces

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This write-up documents a systematic scan of Windows 11 local privilege escalation “configuration surface” (tasks, pipes, services, COM, registry loadpoints, WMI, device ACLs), explicitly excluding kernel/driver memory corruption exploits.  
- The primary confirmed finding is a Microsoft-signed scheduled task (`\Microsoft\Windows\Application Experience\MareBackup`) that runs as SYSTEM but has a task definition file whose embedded SDDL grants `BUILTIN\Users` `GenericAll`, enabling any standard user to overwrite the XML and gain SYSTEM code execution on Task Scheduler reload/reboot.  
- A secondary finding is a world-writable named pipe (`\\.\pipe\WiFiNetworkManagerTask`) where `WlanSvc` trusts a client-supplied SID at a fixed offset, allowing SID spoofing (e.g., claiming `S-1-5-18`), though no full EoP was completed.  
- A third finding flags a NULL-DACL named pipe exposed by Realtek’s HD Audio Universal Service running as LocalSystem, presenting a broad attack surface pending protocol reverse engineering.  
- The author reports that most other common LPE vectors (service ACLs/binaries, autoruns, WMI persistence, COM registration, device IOCTLs like KSecDD/PEAuth/Ahcache) were properly ACL’d or gated, making `MareBackup` the standout misconfiguration.  
- Useful for red teamers/pentesters hunting reliable SYSTEM persistence primitives and for defenders validating task/pipe ACL hygiene on Windows endpoints.

Technical Focus:
- Scheduled Task security descriptors (SDDL) and task XML overwrite for SYSTEM execution
- Named pipe DACL/SDDL analysis and client identity/SID trust flaws
- LocalSystem service attack surface via NULL-DACL IPC endpoints
- Windows LPE surface enumeration methodology (services, COM, registry, WMI, AppX, device objects)
- Kernel device IOCTL surface triage and access gating (KSecDD, PEAuth, Bfs, Ahcache)

Use Cases:
- Identify and exploit user-writable SYSTEM scheduled tasks for delayed SYSTEM execution/persistence
- Audit enterprise Windows images for misconfigured task definition ACLs and embedded SDDL issues
- Hunt for world-writable/NULL-DACL named pipes and test for authentication/impersonation design flaws
- Prioritize reverse engineering targets (vendor LocalSystem services with exposed IPC)
- Build/validate defensive detections around task file tampering and suspicious pipe clients

Keywords:
Windows 11, local privilege escalation, LPE, scheduled tasks, Task Scheduler, MareBackup, SDDL, DACL, GenericAll, BUILTIN\\Users, SYSTEM, task XML, C:\\Windows\\System32\\Tasks, named pipes, WlanSvc, WiFiNetworkManagerTask, SID spoofing, S-1-5-18, NULL DACL, Realtek HD Audio Universal Service, RtkAudUServiceNamedPipe, COM CLSID, KSecDD, PEAuth, IOCTL, device ACLs, persistence